From: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] rpmsg: virtio: rate-limit the "no used buffer" message

rpmsg_recv_done() logs at dev_err level whenever it is entered with no
used buffer. That is not necessarily an error: a remoteproc driver may
legitimately force the vring callback in order to poll for work.

drivers/remoteproc/cix_dsp_rproc.c does exactly that. The DSP runs
rpmsg-lite, which places TX messages in the avail ring rather than the
used ring, so vring_interrupt() cannot detect them and the driver
force-fires the callback on a timer. Every empty poll produced one
dev_err line -- measured at ~16 lines/second on CIX Sky1 (O6N), which
buries the journal and costs a printk per poll.

Rate-limit the message. The diagnostic remains available for the case it
was written for (a genuinely spurious device signal) without flooding.

Signed-off-by: Jason Perlow <jperlow@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/rpmsg/virtio_rpmsg_bus.c	2026-08-15 15:12:34.515121964 +0000
+++ b/drivers/rpmsg/virtio_rpmsg_bus.c	2026-08-15 18:17:41.525199601 +0000
@@ -730,7 +730,17 @@
 
 	msg = virtqueue_get_buf(rvq, &len);
 	if (!msg) {
-		dev_err(dev, "uhm, incoming signal, but no used buffer ?\n");
+		/*
+		 * Not necessarily an error: a driver may legitimately force this
+		 * callback to poll for work. cix_dsp_rproc does exactly that --
+		 * rpmsg-lite on the DSP puts TX messages in the avail ring rather
+		 * than the used ring, so vring_interrupt() cannot see them and the
+		 * driver force-fires the callback on a timer. Each empty poll then
+		 * printed at dev_err level, flooding the log at ~16 lines/s on
+		 * CIX Sky1. Rate-limit it: the diagnostic is still available, the
+		 * flood is not.
+		 */
+		dev_err_ratelimited(dev, "uhm, incoming signal, but no used buffer ?\n");
 		return;
 	}
 
